1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,193
Hi.

2
00:00:00,193 --> 00:00:05,463
In this video I will show you, step by step, how I built the Hybrid Intent Router.

3
00:00:05,463 --> 00:00:10,284
It is a small multilingual model that understands what a customer wants, in

4
00:00:10,284 --> 00:00:12,019
English, Hindi and Kannada.

5
00:00:12,019 --> 00:00:15,747
When it is confident, it answers in milliseconds on a CPU.

6
00:00:15,747 --> 00:00:18,768
When it is not, it asks a large language model.

7
00:00:18,768 --> 00:00:23,974
I will show the real code, the real commands, and the real results from this run.

8
00:00:24,674 --> 00:00:28,232
A voice bot first has to understand what the customer wants.

9
00:00:28,232 --> 00:00:29,655
We call that the intent.

10
00:00:29,655 --> 00:00:33,272
For example, "wake me up at five am" is the intent alarm set.

11
00:00:33,272 --> 00:00:37,244
The easy answer is to send every message to a large language model.

12
00:00:37,244 --> 00:00:41,217
But every call is a network round trip, and every call costs money.

13
00:00:41,217 --> 00:00:45,130
A small model is fast and free to run, but it makes more mistakes.

14
00:00:45,130 --> 00:00:49,933
And most labelled training data is in English, while many users write in Hindi or

15
00:00:49,933 --> 00:00:50,407
Kannada.

16
00:00:51,107 --> 00:00:52,386
So here is the idea.

17
00:00:52,386 --> 00:00:55,200
Every message goes to the small model first.

18
00:00:55,200 --> 00:00:58,972
It returns an intent and a confidence between zero and one.

19
00:00:58,972 --> 00:01:04,151
If the confidence is above a threshold, which I call t, we keep the small model's

20
00:01:04,151 --> 00:01:04,599
answer.

21
00:01:04,599 --> 00:01:07,732
If it is below t, we send the message to the LLM.

22
00:01:07,732 --> 00:01:10,993
The whole project is about measuring this honestly.

23
00:01:10,993 --> 00:01:14,254
How many messages can the small model handle alone?

24
00:01:14,254 --> 00:01:15,852
What happens to accuracy?

25
00:01:15,852 --> 00:01:18,474
And how much time and money does it save?

26
00:01:19,174 --> 00:01:24,493
Before writing any code, I checked the machine and saved the output to a log file.

27
00:01:24,493 --> 00:01:29,748
The project runs in WSL2 Ubuntu, because gunicorn does not run on native Windows.

28
00:01:29,748 --> 00:01:35,132
There are 12 CPU cores and 7.4 gigabytes of RAM, which later decide how many server

29
00:01:35,132 --> 00:01:36,300
workers I can run.

30
00:01:36,300 --> 00:01:41,230
The laptop also has a small NVIDIA GPU with 4 gigabytes, which is enough for

31
00:01:41,230 --> 00:01:41,814
training.

32
00:01:41,814 --> 00:01:47,004
The very first commit was the gitignore file, so the API key in the dot env file

33
00:01:47,004 --> 00:01:49,274
can never be committed by accident.

34
00:01:49,974 --> 00:01:50,921
Next, research.

35
00:01:50,921 --> 00:01:56,034
I looked up every fact I needed and saved each one with

36
00:01:56,034 --> 00:01:57,864
its source link and the date.

37
00:01:57,864 --> 00:01:59,758
The dataset is Amazon MASSIVE.

38
00:01:59,758 --> 00:02:04,492
The model is MuRIL, which is a BERT style model trained on seventeen Indian

39
00:02:04,492 --> 00:02:05,880
languages and English.

40
00:02:05,880 --> 00:02:10,867
Published results show it transfers to Hindi and Kannada better than XLM-R, the

41
00:02:10,867 --> 00:02:12,129
more popular choice.

42
00:02:12,129 --> 00:02:16,863
For the LLM, I picked Gemini 3.5 Flash Lite, because it is cheap, fast, and

43
00:02:16,863 --> 00:02:18,883
supports structured JSON output.

44
00:02:19,583 --> 00:02:22,624
MASSIVE is a voice assistant dataset from Amazon.

45
00:02:22,624 --> 00:02:27,652
Each sentence was translated into fifty-one languages, and the translations share

46
00:02:27,652 --> 00:02:28,396
the same id.

47
00:02:28,396 --> 00:02:29,638
That is very useful.

48
00:02:29,638 --> 00:02:34,541
It means I can test Hindi and Kannada on exactly the same sentences as English.

49
00:02:34,541 --> 00:02:39,444
The make data command downloads the official file, checks its SHA-256 hash, and

50
00:02:39,444 --> 00:02:43,416
writes clean files with the text, the intent and a number label.

51
00:02:44,116 --> 00:02:49,293
The free tier of the LLM is limited, so the LLM cannot see all five thousand test

52
00:02:49,293 --> 00:02:51,147
sentences in three languages.

53
00:02:51,147 --> 00:02:53,512
I made two fixed subsets with a seed.

54
00:02:53,512 --> 00:02:57,155
Dev sub has 300 sentences and is used for every decision.

55
00:02:57,155 --> 00:03:01,373
Test sub has 500 sentences and is used only for the final numbers.

56
00:03:01,373 --> 00:03:06,486
The sampling is stratified, which means each intent gets a share proportional to

57
00:03:06,486 --> 00:03:07,061
its size.

58
00:03:07,061 --> 00:03:12,238
Nineteen unit tests check sizes, labels, parallel ids, and that the subsets never

59
00:03:12,238 --> 00:03:12,750
overlap.

60
00:03:13,450 --> 00:03:16,988
Phase three is the baseline: send everything to the LLM.

61
00:03:16,988 --> 00:03:19,326
I used the official google-genai SDK.

62
00:03:19,326 --> 00:03:24,317
The response must be JSON, and the intent field is an enum, which means a fixed

63
00:03:24,317 --> 00:03:25,897
list of the sixty labels.

64
00:03:25,897 --> 00:03:27,792
I still validate every answer.

65
00:03:27,792 --> 00:03:33,037
If a label is wrong, I retry once, and then count it as invalid, which means wrong.

66
00:03:33,037 --> 00:03:38,028
Rate limits and server errors are retried with exponential backoff, so the wait

67
00:03:38,028 --> 00:03:39,166
doubles each time.

68
00:03:39,866 --> 00:03:42,576
Every LLM answer is written to a cache file.

69
00:03:42,576 --> 00:03:47,564
The key is the model, the prompt version, the mode, the language and the sentence

70
00:03:47,564 --> 00:03:47,749
id.

71
00:03:47,749 --> 00:03:52,861
So if a run stops half way, I just run it again, and only the missing sentences are

72
00:03:52,861 --> 00:03:53,169
sent.

73
00:03:53,169 --> 00:03:58,096
The cache is also committed to git, so anyone can reproduce the routing analysis

74
00:03:58,096 --> 00:03:59,266
without an API key.

75
00:03:59,966 --> 00:04:02,830
I tried two prompts on sixty dev sentences.

76
00:04:02,830 --> 00:04:05,494
The first gives only the list of labels.

77
00:04:05,494 --> 00:04:10,756
The second also gives one short English example for each intent, taken from the

78
00:04:10,756 --> 00:04:11,688
training data.

79
00:04:11,688 --> 00:04:16,151
The version with examples scored 86.7%, against 80.0% without them.

80
00:04:16,151 --> 00:04:19,148
So version two was frozen for all later runs.

81
00:04:19,148 --> 00:04:21,346
This decision used dev data only.

82
00:04:21,346 --> 00:04:24,277
The test data stays untouched until the end.

83
00:04:24,977 --> 00:04:30,311
Then the full run: three hundred plus five hundred sentences, in three languages.

84
00:04:30,311 --> 00:04:34,723
That is two thousand four hundred answers, sent twenty per request.

85
00:04:34,723 --> 00:04:39,728
The LLM scored 84.2% on dev sub and 84.1% on test sub, pooled over the three

86
00:04:39,728 --> 00:04:40,386
languages.

87
00:04:40,386 --> 00:04:42,691
It never returned an invalid label.

88
00:04:42,691 --> 00:04:46,708
Notice that it is almost equally good in all three languages.

89
00:04:46,708 --> 00:04:49,277
That is the bar the router has to meet.

90
00:04:49,977 --> 00:04:51,772
Two real errors happened here.

91
00:04:51,772 --> 00:04:56,618
First, a read timeout from the HTTP library was not caught by my retry logic, and

92
00:04:56,618 --> 00:04:58,114
it crashed the whole run.

93
00:04:58,114 --> 00:05:02,422
I mapped all network errors to a retried error, and wrote a test for it.

94
00:05:02,422 --> 00:05:06,311
Second, the Linux virtual machine stopped in the middle of a run.

95
00:05:06,311 --> 00:05:11,157
The cache file ended with two thousand zero bytes, and the next run crashed while

96
00:05:11,157 --> 00:05:11,815
reading it.

97
00:05:11,815 --> 00:05:13,251
The fix was at the root.

98
00:05:13,251 --> 00:05:16,004
Every write is now flushed and synced to disk.

99
00:05:16,004 --> 00:05:20,790
And the loader removes crash padding, but still fails loudly on any other broken

100
00:05:20,790 --> 00:05:23,243
line, so real corruption is never hidden.

101
00:05:23,943 --> 00:05:29,766
Batching is good for the free tier, but a real bot sends one message at a time.

102
00:05:29,766 --> 00:05:32,788
So I also sent fifty messages one by one.

103
00:05:32,788 --> 00:05:38,831
The median latency was 1344 ms, and the slowest five percent took 9114 ms or more.

104
00:05:38,831 --> 00:05:44,285
Each call used about 890 input tokens, mostly the label list and examples.

105
00:05:44,285 --> 00:05:48,486
These real token counts are what I use for the cost math.

106
00:05:49,186 --> 00:05:50,999
Phase four: the small model.

107
00:05:50,999 --> 00:05:56,050
I load the MuRIL encoder, and add my own classification head: dropout, and one

108
00:05:56,050 --> 00:05:58,899
linear layer from 768 numbers to 60 intents.

109
00:05:58,899 --> 00:06:02,071
The first token's vector summarises the sentence.

110
00:06:02,071 --> 00:06:05,374
The head turns it into sixty scores, called logits.

111
00:06:05,374 --> 00:06:08,093
The highest score is the predicted intent.

112
00:06:08,093 --> 00:06:12,820
Writing the head myself keeps the PyTorch part clear and easy to explain.

113
00:06:13,520 --> 00:06:15,975
I did not use the Hugging Face Trainer.

114
00:06:15,975 --> 00:06:20,949
I wrote the loop: get a batch, forward pass, cross entropy loss, backward pass,

115
00:06:20,949 --> 00:06:23,341
clip the gradients, and an AdamW step.

116
00:06:23,341 --> 00:06:28,503
The learning rate warms up for the first ten percent of steps, then goes down in a

117
00:06:28,503 --> 00:06:29,385
straight line.

118
00:06:29,385 --> 00:06:31,022
I trained on English only.

119
00:06:31,022 --> 00:06:35,995
After every epoch I measure English dev accuracy, keep the best checkpoint, and

120
00:06:35,995 --> 00:06:38,702
stop if it does not improve for two epochs.

121
00:06:38,702 --> 00:06:41,158
The big word embedding table is frozen.

122
00:06:41,158 --> 00:06:45,691
It saves GPU memory and keeps the shared multilingual vocabulary intact.

123
00:06:46,391 --> 00:06:51,830
Before the full run, a thirty step smoke run estimated the training time: about 5.6

124
00:06:51,830 --> 00:06:52,355
minutes.

125
00:06:52,355 --> 00:06:55,369
Far below my four hour limit, so I kept MuRIL.

126
00:06:55,369 --> 00:06:57,794
But the first real run had a problem.

127
00:06:57,794 --> 00:07:02,316
After five epochs, English dev accuracy was still going up, at 77.4%.

128
00:07:02,316 --> 00:07:04,675
The model had not finished learning.

129
00:07:04,675 --> 00:07:09,722
So I raised the limit to fifteen epochs and let early stopping on English dev

130
00:07:09,722 --> 00:07:10,180
decide.

131
00:07:10,180 --> 00:07:13,457
I kept the first run's log, to be honest about it.

132
00:07:14,157 --> 00:07:16,486
Here is the final training curve.

133
00:07:16,486 --> 00:07:19,732
The blue line is the training loss going down.

134
00:07:19,732 --> 00:07:22,554
The purple line is English dev accuracy.

135
00:07:22,554 --> 00:07:26,012
The best epoch was 12, with 87.9% on English dev.

136
00:07:26,012 --> 00:07:29,540
The whole run took 14.0 minutes on the laptop GPU.

137
00:07:30,240 --> 00:07:31,980
Now the interesting part.

138
00:07:31,980 --> 00:07:36,017
On the full English test set the small model scores 88.1%.

139
00:07:36,017 --> 00:07:40,054
On Hindi, which it never saw in training, it scores 77.9%.

140
00:07:40,054 --> 00:07:41,307
On Kannada, 70.7%.

141
00:07:41,307 --> 00:07:44,160
This is zero-shot cross-lingual transfer.

142
00:07:44,160 --> 00:07:49,937
MuRIL learned during pre-training that a Hindi sentence and its English translation

143
00:07:49,937 --> 00:07:54,670
mean the same thing, so an English classifier partly works in Hindi.

144
00:07:54,670 --> 00:07:59,751
The published MuRIL numbers are higher, partly because those papers chose

145
00:07:59,751 --> 00:08:01,421
checkpoints differently.

146
00:08:01,421 --> 00:08:05,040
I only used English dev, to keep it truly zero-shot.

147
00:08:05,740 --> 00:08:10,119
A threshold only makes sense if the confidence means something.

148
00:08:10,119 --> 00:08:15,679
If the model says ninety percent, it should be right about ninety percent of the

149
00:08:15,679 --> 00:08:16,026
time.

150
00:08:16,026 --> 00:08:19,501
Temperature scaling fixes this with one number, T.

151
00:08:19,501 --> 00:08:22,837
I divide all the scores by T before the softmax.

152
00:08:22,837 --> 00:08:26,868
The predicted intent does not change, only the confidence.

153
00:08:26,868 --> 00:08:30,274
T was fitted on English dev and came out as 1.34.

154
00:08:30,274 --> 00:08:35,208
The expected calibration error, called E C E, went from 0.076 to 0.016.

155
00:08:35,208 --> 00:08:36,320
Lower is better.

156
00:08:37,020 --> 00:08:38,295
Now the threshold.

157
00:08:38,295 --> 00:08:43,887
I tried every value from zero to one, in steps of one hundredth, plus one point

158
00:08:43,887 --> 00:08:47,002
zero one, which sends everything to the LLM.

159
00:08:47,002 --> 00:08:52,807
The rule was written down before looking at any test data: pick the threshold with

160
00:08:52,807 --> 00:08:58,400
the fewest LLM calls, while the router stays within one percentage point of the

161
00:08:58,400 --> 00:09:00,453
LLM-only accuracy on dev sub.

162
00:09:00,453 --> 00:09:04,701
On dev, the LLM alone scored 84.2%, so the target was 83.2%.

163
00:09:04,701 --> 00:09:06,754
The chosen threshold is 0.56.

164
00:09:07,454 --> 00:09:09,638
This chart shows the whole trade-off.

165
00:09:09,638 --> 00:09:12,530
On the left is dev sub, on the right is test sub.

166
00:09:12,530 --> 00:09:16,603
Each line is one language, and the dashed line is all three together.

167
00:09:16,603 --> 00:09:19,672
Moving right means sending more messages to the LLM.

168
00:09:19,672 --> 00:09:21,738
The dots show the chosen threshold.

169
00:09:21,738 --> 00:09:23,214
Look at the English line.

170
00:09:23,214 --> 00:09:28,113
With almost nothing sent to the LLM, it is already high, and a few LLM answers push

171
00:09:28,113 --> 00:09:28,998
it even higher.

172
00:09:28,998 --> 00:09:33,956
Hindi and Kannada start lower and climb fast as their unsure messages go to the LLM.

173
00:09:33,956 --> 00:09:37,144
Notice that the test curve peaks around forty percent.

174
00:09:37,144 --> 00:09:39,446
The two models make different mistakes.

175
00:09:39,446 --> 00:09:44,286
My rule chose the fewest LLM calls instead, because that was the goal I wrote down

176
00:09:44,286 --> 00:09:45,820
before seeing any results.

177
00:09:46,520 --> 00:09:52,659
Here are the final numbers on test sub, all three languages together, with the

178
00:09:52,659 --> 00:09:54,627
threshold fixed from dev.

179
00:09:54,627 --> 00:09:56,909
The small model alone: 78.6%.

180
00:09:56,909 --> 00:09:58,562
The LLM alone: 84.1%.

181
00:09:58,562 --> 00:10:03,835
The router: 84.2%, while sending only 11.1% of messages to the LLM.

182
00:10:03,835 --> 00:10:10,368
So the router matches the LLM-only accuracy, with about one LLM call for every nine

183
00:10:10,368 --> 00:10:11,076
messages.

184
00:10:11,076 --> 00:10:13,516
The accuracy change is +0.1 pp.

185
00:10:13,516 --> 00:10:19,261
The oracle, which is right whenever either model is right, reaches 91.5%.

186
00:10:19,261 --> 00:10:23,354
It shows how much a perfect router could still gain.

187
00:10:24,054 --> 00:10:26,789
Per language, the picture makes sense.

188
00:10:26,789 --> 00:10:32,259
English is the language the small model was trained on, so it keeps the most

189
00:10:32,259 --> 00:10:34,706
messages: only 3.8% go to the LLM.

190
00:10:34,706 --> 00:10:37,800
Hindi sends 13.0%, and Kannada sends 16.4%.

191
00:10:37,800 --> 00:10:43,486
The less the small model knows a language, the more often it is unsure, and the

192
00:10:43,486 --> 00:10:44,998
router asks for help.

193
00:10:45,698 --> 00:10:48,295
Speed matters for a voice bot.

194
00:10:48,295 --> 00:10:55,308
On the CPU, with four threads, the small model answers one message in a median of

195
00:10:55,308 --> 00:10:58,772
38 ms, against 1344 ms for one LLM call.

196
00:10:58,772 --> 00:11:00,417
Now money and time.

197
00:11:00,417 --> 00:11:07,516
Using the paid price of the model and the real token counts, the LLM costs $0.2991

198
00:11:07,516 --> 00:11:09,421
per thousand messages.

199
00:11:09,421 --> 00:11:11,586
The router costs $0.0331.

200
00:11:11,586 --> 00:11:18,253
The router avoids 88.9% of LLM calls, and the typical time per message, using

201
00:11:18,253 --> 00:11:21,976
median times, drops from 1344 ms to 186 ms.

202
00:11:21,976 --> 00:11:26,565
The accuracy change against the LLM alone is +0.1 pp.

203
00:11:27,265 --> 00:11:29,401
Here are eight real test messages.

204
00:11:29,401 --> 00:11:33,549
The top four stayed with the small model because it was confident.

205
00:11:33,549 --> 00:11:35,560
The bottom four went to the LLM.

206
00:11:35,560 --> 00:11:40,337
You can see English, Hindi and Kannada sentences, the true intent, the small

207
00:11:40,337 --> 00:11:43,542
model's guess and confidence, and the final answer.

208
00:11:44,242 --> 00:11:46,705
Phase six is the API, built with Flask.

209
00:11:46,705 --> 00:11:51,757
The model is loaded once when the app starts, and the threshold is read from the

210
00:11:51,757 --> 00:11:53,083
routing results file.

211
00:11:53,083 --> 00:11:55,672
The predict endpoint validates the input.

212
00:11:55,672 --> 00:12:00,914
Empty text, or more than five hundred characters, returns an error 400 with a clear

213
00:12:00,914 --> 00:12:01,419
message.

214
00:12:01,419 --> 00:12:04,767
If the LLM fails or times out, the app never crashes.

215
00:12:04,767 --> 00:12:08,808
It returns the small model's answer and marks it small fallback.

216
00:12:09,508 --> 00:12:12,082
Here is the smoke test with real requests.

217
00:12:12,082 --> 00:12:15,022
One English sentence, one Hindi and one Kannada.

218
00:12:15,022 --> 00:12:19,985
Each response shows the intent, the confidence, where it was routed, and how long

219
00:12:19,985 --> 00:12:20,475
it took.

220
00:12:21,175 --> 00:12:23,885
Flask's own server is only for development.

221
00:12:23,885 --> 00:12:29,053
In production, a request goes from the browser to NGINX, then to gunicorn, then to

222
00:12:29,053 --> 00:12:32,078
the Flask app, and then to the model or the LLM.

223
00:12:32,078 --> 00:12:34,410
Gunicorn runs three worker processes.

224
00:12:34,410 --> 00:12:39,514
Each one uses four PyTorch threads, so together they use the twelve cores without

225
00:12:39,514 --> 00:12:40,712
fighting over them.

226
00:12:40,712 --> 00:12:41,972
NGINX sits in front.

227
00:12:41,972 --> 00:12:47,077
It handles slow clients, limits the request size, and can serve many connections.

228
00:12:47,777 --> 00:12:51,732
I ran NGINX as a normal user, so no sudo password is needed.

229
00:12:51,732 --> 00:12:54,105
The trick is a project-local config.

230
00:12:54,105 --> 00:12:59,445
The pid file and every temp folder live inside the project, because a normal user

231
00:12:59,445 --> 00:13:01,752
cannot write to the system folders.

232
00:13:01,752 --> 00:13:05,444
It listens on port 8080 and passes requests to gunicorn.

233
00:13:06,144 --> 00:13:08,056
Then a load test with Locust.

234
00:13:08,056 --> 00:13:13,330
20 simulated users sent real test sentences through NGINX, with the LLM switched

235
00:13:13,330 --> 00:13:15,307
off so no API quota was spent.

236
00:13:15,307 --> 00:13:19,329
The server handled 41.2 requests per second, with 0 failures.

237
00:13:19,329 --> 00:13:24,801
Half of the requests finished within 180 ms, and ninety-five percent within 260 ms.

238
00:13:24,801 --> 00:13:26,515
The bottleneck is the CPU.

239
00:13:26,515 --> 00:13:31,921
One message alone takes about 38 ms, but under load the twelve cores are shared by

240
00:13:31,921 --> 00:13:36,272
three workers and the load generator, so requests wait in a queue.

241
00:13:36,272 --> 00:13:41,084
To go faster I would quantize the model or add CPU, not add more workers.

242
00:13:41,084 --> 00:13:42,864
One real bug happened here.

243
00:13:42,864 --> 00:13:45,567
The first load test showed zero requests.

244
00:13:45,567 --> 00:13:50,973
The workers had inherited a broken PyTorch thread pool from the master process and

245
00:13:50,973 --> 00:13:51,369
froze.

246
00:13:51,369 --> 00:13:56,775
The fix was one line: keep the master single-threaded, and let each worker set its

247
00:13:56,775 --> 00:13:58,621
own threads after it starts.

248
00:13:59,321 --> 00:14:01,224
Phase eight is the quality gate.

249
00:14:01,224 --> 00:14:05,923
One command, make verify, runs the linter, every unit test, and a number check.

250
00:14:05,923 --> 00:14:07,707
The number check is important.

251
00:14:07,707 --> 00:14:12,346
Every results table in the readme and the report is generated from the results

252
00:14:12,346 --> 00:14:12,763
folder.

253
00:14:12,763 --> 00:14:15,737
If anyone edits a number by hand, the check fails.

254
00:14:15,737 --> 00:14:20,376
Then I had the repo reviewed independently for test leakage, secrets and

255
00:14:20,376 --> 00:14:21,566
broken instructions.

256
00:14:22,266 --> 00:14:27,644
If an interviewer asks you to explain the project in thirty seconds, say this.

257
00:14:27,644 --> 00:14:30,471
I built an intent router for a voice bot.

258
00:14:30,471 --> 00:14:36,125
A small multilingual model, fine-tuned in PyTorch on English only, answers when it

259
00:14:36,125 --> 00:14:37,021
is confident.

260
00:14:37,021 --> 00:14:39,573
Otherwise the message goes to an LLM.

261
00:14:39,573 --> 00:14:45,158
On Hindi and Kannada test data, the router gets 84.2% accuracy while sending only

262
00:14:45,158 --> 00:14:47,157
11.1% of messages to the LLM.

263
00:14:47,157 --> 00:14:51,432
I served it with Flask, gunicorn and NGINX and load-tested it.

264
00:14:52,132 --> 00:14:52,935
Question one.

265
00:14:52,935 --> 00:14:55,589
Why route, instead of always using the LLM?

266
00:14:55,589 --> 00:14:57,503
Because most messages are easy.

267
00:14:57,503 --> 00:15:02,565
The small model handles them for free in milliseconds, and we pay for the LLM only

268
00:15:02,565 --> 00:15:03,615
on the hard ones.

269
00:15:03,615 --> 00:15:04,418
Question two.

270
00:15:04,418 --> 00:15:06,702
Why choose the threshold on dev data?

271
00:15:06,702 --> 00:15:11,764
Because if you tune on test data, your test score is no longer an honest estimate.

272
00:15:11,764 --> 00:15:13,554
It looks better than reality.

273
00:15:13,554 --> 00:15:14,480
Question three.

274
00:15:14,480 --> 00:15:17,135
Why does English training work for Kannada?

275
00:15:17,135 --> 00:15:21,950
MuRIL was pre-trained on many languages together, including translation pairs.

276
00:15:21,950 --> 00:15:26,519
So similar meanings get similar vectors in every language, and the English

277
00:15:26,519 --> 00:15:28,432
classifier partly carries over.

278
00:15:29,132 --> 00:15:30,017
Question four.

279
00:15:30,017 --> 00:15:32,102
What does temperature scaling do?

280
00:15:32,102 --> 00:15:37,219
It divides the scores by one number before the softmax, so the confidence matches

281
00:15:37,219 --> 00:15:38,357
the real accuracy.

282
00:15:38,357 --> 00:15:40,378
It never changes the prediction.

283
00:15:40,378 --> 00:15:41,263
Question five.

284
00:15:41,263 --> 00:15:42,716
What is the bottleneck?

285
00:15:42,716 --> 00:15:44,359
CPU-bound model inference.

286
00:15:44,359 --> 00:15:49,476
Each worker answers one request at a time, so throughput is limited by the cores,

287
00:15:49,476 --> 00:15:50,866
not by NGINX or Flask.

288
00:15:50,866 --> 00:15:51,751
And what next?

289
00:15:51,751 --> 00:15:56,995
Quantize the model to make it faster, add a little Hindi and Kannada training data,

290
00:15:56,995 --> 00:15:59,332
and test with real code-mixed speech.

291
00:16:00,032 --> 00:16:01,537
That is the whole project.

292
00:16:01,537 --> 00:16:05,879
A small model trained on English, a big model for the hard cases, an honest

293
00:16:05,879 --> 00:16:07,557
threshold, and a real server.

294
00:16:07,557 --> 00:16:12,130
The readme, the final report and the interview guide are in the repository, and

295
00:16:12,130 --> 00:16:15,082
every number in them comes from the results folder.

296
00:16:15,082 --> 00:16:16,413
Thank you for watching.
